`display: contents` does **not** suppress pseudo-elements. The element itself generates no box, but its children and pseudo-elements—such as `::before` and `::after`—should still generate boxes normally.

```css
.wrapper {
  display: contents;
}

.wrapper::before {
  content: "";
  display: block;
}
```

If the pseudo-element has no generated box, check its `content` property. When `content` is applied to a pseudo-element, the pseudo-element is not generated if it does not produce content.

Also note that the W3C specification states that `display: contents` was not implemented correctly in major browsers at the time of the cited result, so browser behavior may differ.
